WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:03.920
Packaging is one of my favorite areas&nbsp;
in design — it’s highly profitable,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:03.920 --> 00:00:08.640
great for portfolio, and it’s quite fast to&nbsp;
deliver for how much you can charge. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:08.640 --> 00:00:12.240
it also requires effort, decision&nbsp;
making and creative thinking.

00:00:12.240 --> 00:00:15.680
So today, I’ll walk you through&nbsp;
my entire process of packaging&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:15.680 --> 00:00:20.560
design for a real product line — from the&nbsp;
client’s first ideas to choosing colors,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.560 --> 00:00:25.200
fonts, and final assets. As a bonus,&nbsp;
I’ll also teach how you can create&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:25.200 --> 00:00:31.120
stunningly beautiful and hyper realistic&nbsp;
3D mockups, that will convince any client.

00:00:31.120 --> 00:00:36.640
Everything is time-stamped, so just skip&nbsp;
what you’re not interested in. Let’s begin!

00:00:36.640 --> 00:00:40.560
This video is sponsored by&nbsp;
Pacdora. More on them later.

00:00:40.560 --> 00:00:47.040
Look, I know you’re not here to read a briefing,&nbsp;
so I’ll be as brief as possible. The first part&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:47.040 --> 00:00:52.400
of any design is always carefully reading the&nbsp;
briefing and understanding what the client wants,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.400 --> 00:00:58.400
I cannot stress this enough. This is the base for&nbsp;
our next step, which will be gathering references.

00:00:58.400 --> 00:01:03.440
Company briefings frequently feel generic&nbsp;
and empty, so it’s your job as a designer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:03.440 --> 00:01:09.280
to talk to the company and understand what they&nbsp;
actually want behind their often vague requests.

00:01:09.280 --> 00:01:13.920
The client for this project is a local tea&nbsp;
company that asked not to be disclosed,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:13.920 --> 00:01:19.040
so I’m using a fake name. They have been&nbsp;
selling common, everyday teas for a few years,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.040 --> 00:01:24.720
but now they want to launch a premium line that&nbsp;
costs more but has higher quality ingredients.

00:01:24.720 --> 00:01:27.600
Their goal is packaging that&nbsp;
feels elegant and authentic,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:27.600 --> 00:01:30.880
but still modern and approachable&nbsp;
— something that would stand out&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:30.880 --> 00:01:35.520
on store shelves without looking&nbsp;
overly traditional or old-fashioned.

00:01:35.520 --> 00:01:41.040
The product line consists of four different&nbsp;
blends, each one with a different base tea:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:41.040 --> 00:01:47.360
black tea, white tea, green tea and chamomile.&nbsp;
Each one needs its own assets and color palettes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:47.360 --> 00:01:51.920
but the line also has to feel cohesive&nbsp;
as a single family of products.

00:01:51.920 --> 00:01:58.000
The target audience is people between 25 and 45&nbsp;
years old — design-conscious consumers who care&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:58.000 --> 00:02:03.680
about what they buy, appreciate natural products,&nbsp;
and are willing to pay a little more for quality.

00:02:03.680 --> 00:02:07.360
So, my challenge is to combine&nbsp;
premium aesthetics with warm,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:07.360 --> 00:02:11.840
organic visuals — a balance&nbsp;
between sophistication and comfort.

00:02:11.840 --> 00:02:14.960
The company also provided me&nbsp;
with the package dimensions,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:14.960 --> 00:02:20.400
so I jumped straight into one of my favorite&nbsp;
tools for packaging design: Pacdora.

00:02:20.400 --> 00:02:25.280
I don’t do a lot of sponsorships on this channel,&nbsp;
but the ones I do are products I actually use,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:25.280 --> 00:02:30.800
and services I really believe and trust. Pacdora&nbsp;
has a huge collection of packaging mockups,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.800 --> 00:02:35.680
templates and dielines that are&nbsp;
100% customizable and downloadable,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:35.680 --> 00:02:41.360
and that’s not even what I like the most: you&nbsp;
can also create incredibly realistic 3D mockups,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:41.360 --> 00:02:45.680
with an editor that’s easy to use, runs&nbsp;
straight on the browser and doesn’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:45.680 --> 00:02:51.360
require any 3D knowledge whatsoever. 3Ds&nbsp;
are rendered using Blender technology and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:51.360 --> 00:02:57.200
look awesome - every render you’ve seen so far&nbsp;
was made using Pacdora. I personally know how&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:57.200 --> 00:03:02.960
to use dedicated 3D softwares, and I still&nbsp;
prefer to use Pacdora for product mockups.

00:03:02.960 --> 00:03:08.960
Using the code ANDY50 you guarantee&nbsp;
a 50% lifetime discount on Pacdora,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:08.960 --> 00:03:14.400
which is actually insane, you will forever pay&nbsp;
half the price of the subscription using my&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:14.400 --> 00:03:19.760
code. Pacdora has sponsored this channel before,&nbsp;
and thousands of people have already subscribed,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:19.760 --> 00:03:23.920
so I’m eager to see you join&nbsp;
them. Now, back to the video.

00:03:23.920 --> 00:03:29.040
The package should be 80 millimeters wide,&nbsp;
80 millimeters tall, and 50 millimeters deep,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:29.040 --> 00:03:33.200
so I searched for a box of the model&nbsp;
I wanted, which is a tuck end box,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:33.200 --> 00:03:36.320
and quickly edited the box dimensions. Like,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:36.320 --> 00:03:42.560
seriously, it was… it was this fast. Now I&nbsp;
can just download the dielines and move on.

00:03:42.560 --> 00:03:47.200
After understanding the briefing, the next&nbsp;
logical step is to search for references.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:47.200 --> 00:03:51.360
It’s critical that you understand the market&nbsp;
you’re designing for. You need to find that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:51.360 --> 00:03:55.920
sweet spot between staying recognizable&nbsp;
as a brand that belongs to that niche, but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:55.920 --> 00:04:01.840
also standing out from all other brands. And you&nbsp;
can only do that if you have enough references.

00:04:01.840 --> 00:04:07.840
I mostly gather my references through Pinterest,&nbsp;
but I also occasionally use Dribbble and Behance.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:07.840 --> 00:04:13.440
I select a couple dozen images of packagings&nbsp;
think will be useful for my designing process,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:13.440 --> 00:04:17.600
things that are in line with the company&nbsp;
briefing and what they expect of the product,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:17.600 --> 00:04:22.080
and keep them open on my second&nbsp;
monitor at all times. I will&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:22.080 --> 00:04:26.720
also occasionally browse Pinterest&nbsp;
along the way to gather more ideas.

00:04:26.720 --> 00:04:31.360
Now, after spending almost a full day going&nbsp;
over the briefing, talking to the company&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:31.360 --> 00:04:36.320
and looking for references, I had a pretty&nbsp;
good idea of what I was going to create.

00:04:36.320 --> 00:04:40.720
My main idea was to create a strong&nbsp;
background using very organic shapes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:40.720 --> 00:04:45.280
meaning lots of curves and no sharp corners,&nbsp;
which will add a very natural feel to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:45.280 --> 00:04:49.840
package. I’ll use 3 different colors for&nbsp;
this background - that gives me flexibility&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:49.840 --> 00:04:54.400
to create distinguishable color palettes&nbsp;
for each product, without adding too much&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:54.400 --> 00:04:58.880
information. Here I’m trying to come up&nbsp;
with a design that not only looks good,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:58.880 --> 00:05:03.920
but is also replicable - both because I&nbsp;
need 4 different designs for this project,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:03.920 --> 00:05:08.160
but also in case the company decides to&nbsp;
expand the product line in the future.

00:05:08.160 --> 00:05:10.640
To complement the colorful&nbsp;
background and the organic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:10.640 --> 00:05:14.400
aesthetic, I want to make stylized&nbsp;
illustrations of the ingredients,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:14.400 --> 00:05:19.040
which will then be used to indicate&nbsp;
the tea blend all across the package.

00:05:19.040 --> 00:05:24.480
Finally, for just a touch of sophistication,&nbsp;
I’ll work with a good, strong serif font,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:24.480 --> 00:05:30.080
and try not to overcrowd the package with&nbsp;
too much info and elements, as white space&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:30.080 --> 00:05:35.760
and minimalism often give the product a&nbsp;
sense of higher quality and premium price.

00:05:35.760 --> 00:05:39.040
Now, to start actually working on&nbsp;
the packaging, I gathered reference&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:39.040 --> 00:05:44.000
photos of all the ingredients I was going to&nbsp;
illustrate. For this step I used Adobe Express,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:44.000 --> 00:05:49.280
which gives me access to assets from Adobe Stock,&nbsp;
and while I know most of you don’t have an Adobe&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:49.280 --> 00:05:54.000
subscription,if you’re someone that needs&nbsp;
a high amount of stock assets regularly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:54.000 --> 00:05:57.600
the premium plan on Express is&nbsp;
only like 10 bucks a month and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:57.600 --> 00:06:03.360
you get access to millions of Adobe Stock&nbsp;
assets, so I think it’s really worth it.

00:06:03.360 --> 00:06:07.760
After gathering all these images I&nbsp;
moved on to Illustrator to create the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:07.760 --> 00:06:12.080
illustrations of the ingredients, which, honestly,&nbsp;
was the most time consuming part of this whole&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:12.080 --> 00:06:17.520
process, it took me 2 whole days of vectorizing&nbsp;
and trying to find the best way to illustrate each&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:17.520 --> 00:06:22.400
ingredient. I have almost 8 hours of footage&nbsp;
just from this part of the process, plus&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:22.400 --> 00:06:27.840
everything I didn’t record, but the end result&nbsp;
was really good, so I’m glad I took the time.

00:06:27.840 --> 00:06:32.880
With all my assets more or less ready, I started&nbsp;
working on the background colors of the packaging,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:32.880 --> 00:06:37.520
which as I mentioned, will be the base&nbsp;
for the entire product line. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:37.520 --> 00:06:42.000
you’ll see that my design process isn’t&nbsp;
nearly as linear as it might sound when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:42.000 --> 00:06:45.920
I’m narrating it here. For instance,&nbsp;
before even touching the colors,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:45.920 --> 00:06:49.920
I actually placed some of the front&nbsp;
elements first, just to get a sense of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:49.920 --> 00:06:55.760
composition — where things should sit, how the&nbsp;
space feels, and how the layout flows overall.

00:06:55.760 --> 00:07:00.880
I don’t really follow a specific order when&nbsp;
I’m designing — it’s not like I decide, now&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:00.880 --> 00:07:06.320
I’m only working on colors, and then I’ll move on&nbsp;
to work only on the text. As ideas come to mind,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:06.320 --> 00:07:11.440
or as I start getting a sense of what might look&nbsp;
good, I just go for it. I don’t like to push&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:11.440 --> 00:07:16.560
ideas aside just because I told myself I was&nbsp;
working on something else and risk forgetting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:16.560 --> 00:07:22.880
a great idea afterwards. So, even if the design&nbsp;
process sometimes looks a bit all over the place,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:22.880 --> 00:07:27.360
that’s just how my brain works — and&nbsp;
honestly, that’s when I’m the most creative.

00:07:27.360 --> 00:07:31.920
One thing I always like to point out about&nbsp;
packaging design is how important it is to be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:31.920 --> 00:07:37.680
mindful of how colors and elements overlap.&nbsp;
It’s easy to think of the design as just a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:37.680 --> 00:07:42.560
flat image on the screen, but in reality,&nbsp;
this will be folded, glued, and assembled&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:42.560 --> 00:07:48.720
later — so we need to understand how each panel&nbsp;
connects and flows into the next. For example,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:48.720 --> 00:07:53.360
the right edge of the layout actually wraps&nbsp;
around and connects to the left side once&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:53.360 --> 00:07:58.320
the box is folded. So I took extra care to&nbsp;
make sure the colors flowed naturally from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:58.320 --> 00:08:03.680
one side to the other — no harsh breaks,&nbsp;
just a continuous line that wraps smoothly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:03.680 --> 00:08:09.520
around the whole package. And that also means&nbsp;
thinking about how those lines flow to the lid,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:09.520 --> 00:08:16.080
both when the box is closed and when it’s opened,&nbsp;
so the design feels intentional from every angle.

00:08:16.080 --> 00:08:20.400
I’ll confess, my brain got really&nbsp;
confused doing this, it was kinda hard,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:20.400 --> 00:08:26.240
but in the end everything worked out just fine&nbsp;
and I was very, very pleased with the result.

00:08:26.240 --> 00:08:31.200
I quickly jumped to Pacdora’s 3D environment to&nbsp;
test out how the colors were looking with the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:31.200 --> 00:08:35.440
box put together, and this is probably&nbsp;
what I love the most about Pacdora,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:35.440 --> 00:08:40.000
how simple it is to get your mockup&nbsp;
ready. I already had my dieline saved,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:40.000 --> 00:08:45.040
so I just had to import an image of the&nbsp;
design and in seconds I had a 3D package&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:45.040 --> 00:08:49.760
in front of me so I could check whether&nbsp;
the elements were placed correctly or not.

00:08:49.760 --> 00:08:54.240
Knowing that everything is perfectly placed&nbsp;
on the layout is super important for me,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:54.240 --> 00:08:57.760
because I want this base layout&nbsp;
to be the same for every package&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:57.760 --> 00:09:02.000
in the product line. The only thing&nbsp;
that’ll change will be the colors.

00:09:02.000 --> 00:09:06.480
So with the background defined, I can start&nbsp;
arranging the other elements across the layout,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:06.480 --> 00:09:10.880
starting with the illustrations. I like&nbsp;
to use the background lines as guides,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:10.880 --> 00:09:16.000
making sure that elements cross the bounds between&nbsp;
colors whenever possible — this helps placing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:16.000 --> 00:09:21.840
the elements in a way that feels balanced, but&nbsp;
not rigid. The idea is to create an “organized&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:21.840 --> 00:09:27.440
randomness” — as if the ingredients were casually&nbsp;
scattered across the package, but still in a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:27.440 --> 00:09:33.760
way that feels intentional. It keeps the layout&nbsp;
organic and natural, without making it look messy.

00:09:33.760 --> 00:09:39.040
This part is really a back-and-forth process:&nbsp;
placing, moving, rotating, and mirroring each&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:39.040 --> 00:09:44.080
element until everything finds its place&nbsp;
and the composition feels just right. It’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:44.080 --> 00:09:50.000
also imperative that the front of the packaging&nbsp;
shows at least one time each of the ingredients.

00:09:50.000 --> 00:09:54.560
Then, throughout the rest of the packaging,&nbsp;
I’ll add the required text sent by the company,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:54.560 --> 00:09:59.680
like weight, ingredients, instructions on&nbsp;
how to brew the tea, the bar code and the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:59.680 --> 00:10:04.960
blank stamping area where the brand can later&nbsp;
print the manufacturing and expiration info.

00:10:04.960 --> 00:10:10.080
I also want to note that the Chinese writing&nbsp;
on the front was a request from the brand,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:10.080 --> 00:10:15.760
it allegedly says “high quality Chinese tea”,&nbsp;
and though I checked on Google Translate,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:17.040 --> 00:10:21.360
I really hope it is not poorly&nbsp;
written or anything like that.

00:10:21.360 --> 00:10:24.640
Finally, once everything found&nbsp;
its place on the packaging,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:24.640 --> 00:10:29.360
we’re ready to see how the finished&nbsp;
product looks by going back to Pacdora.

00:10:29.360 --> 00:10:36.160
And would you look at that, our hard work&nbsp;
has paid off. The packaging looks great,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:36.160 --> 00:10:40.960
elements have a great flow from one panel to&nbsp;
the other and also have a great harmony with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:40.960 --> 00:10:46.000
the colors. Now, I can start working on&nbsp;
the other packages, which, with the base&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:46.000 --> 00:10:50.480
layout ready, should take a fraction of&nbsp;
the time that the first one required.

00:10:50.480 --> 00:10:54.720
With the base layout ready, it was just&nbsp;
a matter changing a couple elements and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:54.720 --> 00:10:59.600
rethinking the color palette for each new&nbsp;
product. It’s not an exaggeration to say&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:59.600 --> 00:11:04.800
that it probably took the same amount of time to&nbsp;
design the first product as it took the next 3.

00:11:04.800 --> 00:11:10.080
The chamomile tea was my favorite of the whole&nbsp;
line, using a warm yellow to reference both&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:10.080 --> 00:11:15.360
the chamomile and the passionfruit, a soft&nbsp;
purple for the lavender and a vibrant green&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:15.360 --> 00:11:20.720
to give it some punch. The illustrations for&nbsp;
this one turned out absolutely stunning and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:20.720 --> 00:11:26.320
the composition overall looks really pretty&nbsp;
and harmonious. I’m really proud of this one.

00:11:26.320 --> 00:11:29.680
For the white tea I used a&nbsp;
cream color for the base,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:29.680 --> 00:11:34.960
which despite not being the color of neither&nbsp;
the white tea leaves or the brewed tea,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:34.960 --> 00:11:40.000
complemented the bright orange and the dark green&nbsp;
really well. The highlight of this packaging&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:40.000 --> 00:11:46.080
is definitely the bergamot and the rosemary&nbsp;
illustrations, which look absolutely gorgeous.

00:11:46.080 --> 00:11:50.400
Finally, for the green tea, I went with&nbsp;
green shades marking both the green tea&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:50.400 --> 00:11:55.680
and the peppermint, and again used a shade&nbsp;
of cream as an accent color in reference&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:55.680 --> 00:12:01.840
to the fennel seed. This is definitely my least&nbsp;
favorite of the bunch, but it still looks great.

00:12:01.840 --> 00:12:06.160
Now, with all packaging design ready, I’ll&nbsp;
make a presentation to the client and wait&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:06.160 --> 00:12:10.960
for their feedback. There will probably&nbsp;
be a back and forth of small changes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:10.960 --> 00:12:16.320
companies rarely, if ever, approve a design&nbsp;
on the first try, but I’m confident that the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:16.320 --> 00:12:20.800
overall design will be approved, since&nbsp;
it’s very in line with that they asked&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:20.800 --> 00:12:25.200
and what their target audience wants.&nbsp;
However, for the sake of this video,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:25.200 --> 00:12:31.840
the design part is finished. Now, we’ll move on to&nbsp;
my favorite part, which is creating the mockups.

00:12:31.840 --> 00:12:36.720
Creating mockups on Pacdora is quite simple,&nbsp;
and I’ll walk you through the process from the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:36.720 --> 00:12:42.080
very beginning, when we selected our dieline.&nbsp;
We’ll browse the model of package we want,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:42.080 --> 00:12:46.720
customize the size if we need, and when&nbsp;
we have the dieline the way we want,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:46.720 --> 00:12:53.440
we’ll go to the menu, give it a name, and click&nbsp;
on Create New Project. Now, this dieline project&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:53.440 --> 00:12:59.760
is saved on our account - we can check that by&nbsp;
going to the menu and clicking on My Projects.

00:12:59.760 --> 00:13:04.160
Back to the dieline, we can download it&nbsp;
by choosing the file format on the right,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:04.160 --> 00:13:07.120
and clicking on “Download the dieline” on the top.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:07.120 --> 00:13:10.480
This dieline is what we’ll&nbsp;
use to create our design.

00:13:10.480 --> 00:13:15.440
Once we have the design ready and saved as&nbsp;
a PNG image, we’ll go to “Design Online” on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:15.440 --> 00:13:20.480
the dieline page, which will take us to the&nbsp;
mockup editor with a 3D view of the package&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:20.480 --> 00:13:26.240
assembled. However, it is blank, so on the&nbsp;
left, we can upload the image of our design,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:26.240 --> 00:13:31.680
which, since it was made using this very&nbsp;
dieline model, will fit perfectly on the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:31.680 --> 00:13:37.600
mockup. We align the design with the dieline&nbsp;
and scale it if necessary, and hit save.

00:13:37.600 --> 00:13:41.760
And then it’s done! We have the&nbsp;
mockup ready for visualization.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:41.760 --> 00:13:45.600
The slider on the bottom can be&nbsp;
dragged to open or close the box,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:45.600 --> 00:13:49.680
and we can click and drag on the&nbsp;
screen to rotate around the mockup.

00:13:49.680 --> 00:13:52.800
If we click on “3D Design”&nbsp;
at the top of this window,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:52.800 --> 00:13:57.680
we’ll go to the 3D environment where we can&nbsp;
create full scenes and then render our final&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:57.680 --> 00:14:04.160
images. On the left we can load all our models,&nbsp;
add assets, elements and decorations, and change&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:04.160 --> 00:14:09.040
the background color and the lighting. We can&nbsp;
also select the objects on the scene and move,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:09.040 --> 00:14:15.440
rotate and scale them however we want -&nbsp;
the limit here really is our creativity.

00:14:15.440 --> 00:14:18.800
If you find making your own&nbsp;
compositions too difficult,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:18.800 --> 00:14:21.920
pre-made scenes are also available on this menu,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:21.920 --> 00:14:27.760
and you can just select the one you want and&nbsp;
everything will be automatically loaded for you.

00:14:27.760 --> 00:14:32.240
If we need to replace any of the&nbsp;
models, we can re-do the first steps,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:32.240 --> 00:14:38.560
create all the mockups we need, which will then&nbsp;
be saved on our account and show up on this list.

00:14:38.560 --> 00:14:42.960
Once the scene is ready, we can click on&nbsp;
“Super Render”, choose the file format,&nbsp;&nbsp;

